<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/icicles-house.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/icicles-house-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/icicles-house.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/indiana-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Ice Dam, Freeze &amp; Snow-Load Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Ice Dam, Freeze and Snow-Load Claim Appraisal</h1><p class="lede">Indiana winters split two ways: lake-effect snow piles onto roofs in the northern counties, while ice storms coat trees and power lines in the south. Both produce claims that need careful valuation.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>North</dt><dd>Lake-effect snow loads</dd></div><div><dt>South</dt><dd>Ice storms, limbs</dd></div><div><dt>Statewide</dt><dd>Frozen, burst pipes</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why winter claims are an Indiana specialty</h2>
<p>Lake Michigan feeds bands of lake-effect snow into LaPorte, Porter, St. Joseph and Elkhart counties, where seasonal totals routinely exceed what the rest of the state sees. Heavy, wet snow and drifting can overload low-slope roofs, porch roofs and pole barns. Further south, freezing rain is the bigger threat: the January 2009 ice storm brought down trees and power lines across southern Indiana and neighboring states. And statewide, arctic outbreaks such as the polar vortex of January 2014 and the cold snap of January 2019 burst pipes in thousands of homes.</p>
<h2>Ice dams: a water claim that starts on the roof</h2>
<p>When warm attic air melts snow on the upper roof, the water refreezes at the cold eaves and builds a ridge of ice. Meltwater backs up behind it, under shingles and into walls and ceilings. Carriers sometimes treat ice dam leaks as maintenance or ventilation problems. Many policies do cover the resulting water damage, and the amount of that loss can be appraised. We document the ice, the interior water path and the roof edge details together.</p>
<h2>Snow-load and collapse</h2>
<p>Collapse coverage is usually narrower than other perils and is tied to specific causes such as the weight of ice, snow or sleet. We record deflection, cracked rafters or trusses, sagging ceilings and damaged roof sheathing, and we price shoring and engineering where the structure needs it. For a rough sense of the forces involved, try our <a href="/claim-gap-tools/roof-snow-load-estimator/">roof snow load estimator</a>.</p>
<h2>Frozen and burst pipes</h2>
<p>Most homeowners forms cover sudden water damage from a frozen pipe, provided reasonable care was taken to maintain heat or drain the system in a vacant or unoccupied building. We document the burst location, the duration of the leak and the full extent of the water travel, including finished basements below.</p>
<h2>Ice-storm tree damage</h2>
<p>Ice-laden limbs that fall on a home, garage or fence create roof, gutter and framing damage plus tree removal costs that policies often cap. We separate what is covered under the dwelling from the debris removal allowance so each is valued correctly.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is ice dam damage covered in Indiana?</summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on your policy wording. Many homeowners forms cover interior water damage caused by ice dams, while excluding the cost of removing the ice itself. Read the water damage and roof sections of your form.</p></div></details><details><summary>My vacation cabin’s pipes burst while I was away. Will that be covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Policies commonly require reasonable steps to keep heat on or drain the plumbing in unoccupied homes. Records like thermostat logs, utility bills and caretaker visits can matter a great deal.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does my policy cover snow removal from the roof?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually not as a standalone cost, but reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a covered loss may be reimbursable. Keep receipts.</p></div></details></div>
